      "text": " <think>\nTo find the product of 202 and 290, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm. Here's how it works step by step:\n\n1. **Multiply the ones place**: First, multiply the ones digit of each number (2 x 0) and write down the result (0). Then, move to the next digit in the second number (9) and multiply it by the ones digit of the first number (2 x 9 = 18). Write down the result (8) and carry over the 1 to the tens place.\n\n2. **Multiply the tens place**: Now, multiply the tens digit of each number (0 x 2) and write down the result (0). Then, move to the next digit in the second number (9) and multiply it by the tens digit of the first number (0 x 9 = 0). Add the carried over 1 from the previous step to this result (0 + 1 = 1). Write down the result (1) in the tens place.\n\n3. **Multiply the hundreds place**: Finally, multiply the hundreds digit of each number (2 x 2) and write down the result (4). Then, move to the next digit in the second number (9) and multiply it by the hundreds digit of the first number (2 x 9 = 18). Add the carried over 0 from the previous step to this result (18 + 0 = 18). Write down the result (8) in the hundreds place and carry over the 1 to the thousands place.\n\n4. **Multiply the thousands place**: Now, multiply the thousands digit of each number (2 x 2) and write down the result (4). Then, move to the next digit in the second number (9) and multiply it by the thousands digit of the first number (2 x 9 = 18). Add the carried over 1 from the previous step to this result (18 + 1 = 19). Write down the result (9) in the thousands place and carry over the 1 to the ten-thousands place.\n\n5. **Multiply the ten-thousands place**: Finally, multiply the ten-thousands digit of each number (2 x 2) and write down the result (4). Then, move to the next digit in the second number (9) and multiply it by the ten-thousands digit of the first number (2 x 9 = 18). Add the carried over 1 from the previous step to this result (18 + 1 = 19). Write down the result (9) in the ten-thousands place and carry over the 1 to the hundred-thousands place.\n\n6. **Multiply the hundred-thousands place**: Now, multiply the hundred-thousands digit of each number (2 x 2) and write down the result (4). Then, move to the next digit in the second number (9) and multiply it by the hundred-thousands digit of the first number (2 x 9 = 18). Add the carried over 1 from the previous step to this result (18 + 1 = 19). Write down the result (9) in the hundred-thousands place and carry over the 1 to the millions place.\n\n7. **Multiply the millions place**: Finally, multiply the millions digit of each number (2 x 2) and write down the result (4). Then, move to the next digit in the second number (9) and multiply it by the millions digit of the first number (2 x 9 = 18). Add the carried over 1 from the previous step to this result (18 + 1 = 19). Write down the result (9) in the millions place and carry over the 1 to the ten-millions place.\n\n8. **Multiply the ten-millions place**: Now, multiply the ten-millions digit of each number (2 x 2) and write down the result (4). Then, move to the next digit in the second number (9) and multiply it by the ten-millions digit of the first number (2 x 9 = 18). Add the carried over 1 from the previous step to this result (18 + 1 = 19). Write down the result (9) in the ten-millions place and carry over the 1 to the hundred-millions place.\n\n9. **Multiply the hundred-millions place**: Finally, multiply the hundred-millions digit of each number (2 x 2) and write down the result (4). Then, move to the next digit in the second number (9) and multiply it by the hundred-millions digit of the first number (2 x 9 = 18). Add the carried over 1 from the previous step to this result (18 + 1 = 19). Write down the result (9) in the hundred-millions place and carry over the 1 to the billions place.\n\n10. **Multiply the billions place**: Now, multiply the billions digit of each number (2 x 2) an